  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThe 00z San Diego sounding shows a well mixed marine layer in the \nlowest 2,000 feet of the atmosphere with a strong inversion above \nit. Nighttime satellite imagery also shows this as tonight's \nclouds are filled in over a very broad extent, from over the \ncoastal waters up to 20 miles inland throughout SD County and \nOrange County. Clouds are likely to eventually reach into the \nInland Empire in the early morning hours. Communities like \nRiverside, Ontario and possibly San Bernardino will wake up to a \nlow stratus deck overhead. Nighttime temperatures are generally \nrunning 2-7 degrees cooler than yesterday at this time, with \nmorning lows expected to reach below 70 degrees across the coastal\nbasin for the first time in over a week. \n\nToday's temperatures will be similar to yesterday's (maybe a degree \nor two cooler inland) : upper 70s near the beaches, 80s in coastal \nareas, low 90s inland, mid-to-upper 90s in the IE and high desert \nand 100s in the lower deserts.\n\nThe trough over the PacNW which is bringing cooler weather to SoCal \nlooks to move through fairly slowly, maintaining its influence \nthrough Monday. While the main trough elongates over Canada and \nmoves eastward, the GFS and ECMWF show another lobe of cool mid- \nlevel air dropping down straight south out of British Columbia and\nejecting out of the main jet stream. This lobe looks likely to \nbecome a closed upper low over NorCal and Oregon Wednesday and \nThursday, which would keep temperatures close to average despite \nthe ridge over the southeastern US intensifying and expanding \nwestward.\n\nWPC 500mb height clusters indicate high confidence that this \nridge will eventually build Friday and Saturday, increasing temps \naround 5-15 degrees above average into next weekend.\n\nThroughout the forecast period, winds remain likely onshore with a \ncoastal eddy spinning through Wednesday and typical late afternoon \nsouth and west winds over desert slopes.",
